00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:14.910 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  oh  demands for police reform have been  front and center here in 2020  igniting protests in big cities all  across the country and here in las vegas  but leaders at metro police say they've  already embraced across the board  changes on policy  hiring and how officers interact with  the communities they serve  george knapp of our i-team looks at the  changes that have been made  [Music]  in a nondescript warehouse in east las  vegas something heavy is going down  officers cautiously move in on an armed  and troubled man  unsure how this might end elsewhere in  the same building  other officers creep toward an apartment  where an agitated resident is holed up  with a weapon  both scenarios unfold multiple times per  week  as veteran officers re-learn how to  engage  but de-escalate we're a best practice  agency  we are visited by agencies across the  nation  on how we do things sheriff joe lombardo  is proud of how far metro has come in  the last 10 years  las vegas cops have long had a  reputation of being tough customers  before this summer's demonstrations and  demands for reform  metro was ahead of the curve racial  tensions and use of force controversies  have a long history here long before the  george floyd incident  the chokehold death of las vegas charles  bush inflamed passions  in 1992 the aftermath of the rodney king  acquittals  ignited fires and violence here a decade  ago  metro had to confront the ugly numbers  showing  an inordinate number of shootings by  police many of them both black  and unarmed you know we had the doj come  in here and  evaluate us but i think the the  testament to lvmpd is yeah the doj came  in we didn't push back them coming in we  embraced them we asked them to come in  there wasn't a consent decree leveled  against us it was collaborative reform  metro instituted changes from top to  bottom in hiring  training use of force and oversight of  officers  i can pull up a dashboard of every  officer that works for me i can see how  many traffic accidents they've been in  how many use of force issues they've had  non  non-deadly and deadly how many  complaints they've had from citizens  metro's become aggressive in its  recruitment of women and minorities to  join the force  and in its community policing where  officers get to know  the neighborhoods they patrol captain  jim larochelle the policy wonk in the  sheriff's office  says metro has become a model for other  big city police departments  they're at the forefront with their  training addressing scenario based  training where you're  trying to put an officer in an  environment that mirrors the real thing  being better is not good enough as with  george floyd one  bad apple can besmirch an entire agency  metro still gets slapped with lawsuits  almost weekly  justifiably or not it probably will  always be that way  and like i've always said we hire from  the human races all the good bad and  indifferent and faults and bugs and  warts and everything that goes along  with it  and sometimes we can't weed all that out  george knapp 8 news now  while there have been calls for police  reform one major change that took place  at metro police involves banning the  controversial restraint used by some  officers  it is the lateral vascular neck  restraint the department took action in  july to limit its use  nevada legislators banned it during a  special session  the i-team's vanessa murphy takes a  broader look at the number of use of  force incidents  by metro police the department of  justice announced in 2012  the metro police department needed to  change after an extensive review the doj  wrote in this report  the shooting of unarmed suspects remains  a concern  for example between 2007 and 2011 there  were 10  officer involved shootings that involved  unarmed suspects  seven were black i think it's safe to  say they were not in keeping  with best practices the department  implemented multiple changes  in 2019 metro reports out of 16  shootings by police  15 of the subjects were armed there is  an emphasis on de-escalation techniques  in this report analyzing a five-year  period through  2019 the department provides use of  force data  while also acknowledging lessons are  still being learned  it notes four critical incidents in 2019  in which the coroner determined officers  actions  may have contributed to the subject's  cause of death  [Music]  the clark county coroner's office ruled  byron williams death a homicide because  the prone restraint  officers placed him in was a factor in  addition to meth in his system and  health issues  he ran from police after they tried  stopping him for not having a bicycle  light  once they had him in custody williams  told officers he couldn't breathe at  least  24 times multiple officers deactivated  their body cameras at the scene  he deserves justice like what they did  to him was not  was not right it was not fair it was  inhumane the use of force review board  comprised of both commissioned officers  and  three citizens determined officers  should have  immediately rolled williams on his side  after he was handcuffed  and their medical response violated  training and policy  metro took action notifying all officers  to place suspects on their side or sit  them up  so they are not kept in a position like  williams was  and to get medical attention immediately  in a situation like this  the department also updated policy  officers must  keep their body cameras on while at a  scene donovan britt is a citizen  volunteer on the review board we kind of  see these  events and we understand what they  should be doing and something that's  outside of the norm we have we can focus  in on and go why is that the case  the board also makes recommendations on  officer discipline  according to the department out of 31  cases reviewed in 2019  14 officers were disciplined none were  fired and one resigned  captain fred haas works in internal  affairs if there are bad cops out there  i don't want them standing next to me  out there in the community  and throughout the community in 2020  there were black lives matter protests  and calls for police reform while metro  is more advanced than many departments  incidents like william's death  demonstrate there are still serious  flaws  that need to be fixed vanessa murphy  8 news now there is more information for  you on police use of force hirings and  firings and diversity on the department  force  at 8newsnow.com just click on the state  of metro banner  at the top of the page next on politics  now money for education  the hurdle passed by two ballot  initiatives that would raise taxes for  our schools  this is 8 news now  the valley's biggest teachers union has  turned in two ballot initiatives for the  2021 legislative session  it would increase taxes and send that  money to education  initiatives from the clark county  education association target the gaming  tax in the sales tax  so let's explain how a statutory  initiative petition works  both received double the number of  signatures needed about a hundred  thousand  once they're certified the measures go  to the 2021 legislature  if lawmakers do not approve the new  taxes the measures then go to the voters  they would need to approve it in the  2022 general election  now let's look at the initiatives the  first initiative would raise the top  tier of the gaming tax from six point  seven five percent to nine point seven  five percent  the ccea says it would raise about 340  million dollars every year  that money would go into the state's  general fund the second is a 1.5  percentage point increase in the state's  sales tax  here in clark county would go up from  8.35 percent  to 9.875 percent the ccea estimates that  would generate a little over a billion  dollars a year  the hike would be on the portion of the  sales tax known as the local school  support tax  that makes up 2.6 percentage points of  the state's sales tax  and would go up to 4.1 percent funds  raised by that portion of the tax would  then go directly to schools  in what to watch this week we are  keeping an eye on what the governor is  going to do about the coronavirus  governor steve sisolak gave a two-week  deadline to see a drop in cases  or the state would have to add some kind  of mitigation measures  our cases have certainly gone up in that  time frame instead  the governor has not directly said what  extra measures he's considering but  two weeks runs out on tuesday we're  expecting a press conference  in the first part of the week we'll keep  an eye on what the governor says with  regards to the coronavirus and any  possible restrictions  as the week goes on thank you for  watching politics now we will see you  next week have a good rest of the  [Music]  weekend  you

